There will be 3 things running on the master Raspberry PI 4:

rpicam-vid command. This creates a low res and high res stream. The high res stream goes to the network for display on the CRTs. The low res goes to the OK sign detection program.
There is a script in the /home/pi directory rpicam.sh. The guts of it are a command: rpicam-vid -t 0 -n --width 1280  --height 720 --framerate 25 --intra 15 -o - | ffmpeg -i - -vcodec copy -f mpegts -pkt_size 1316 udp://239.0.1.23:1234
There is a daemon rpicam.service that runs on boot to start/restart this script. 
Cam_thumbsup_snapshot.py This python program watches the lowres stream and when it detects a thumbs up sign with a person’s hands, it takes a snapshot and saves it as a jpg
Code is listed below. 
There is a daemon that runs a script that starts this code called thumbsup.service
AsciiArt.py This program watches for the existence of a snapshot jpg, converts it into a txt file and sends it to the printer. When done, it erases both and waits for another. <<not yet implemented. Waiting on a printer>>

Each CRT Raspberry Pi 2 will runs a script called /home/pi/vid_ffmpeg.sh. There is a daemon that starts this script on boot called vidwall.service. The Pis are named CRT1, CRT2, CRT3, CRT4. Additional ones could be added. The active ingredient of the vid_ffmpeg script is 

 ffmpeg -loglevel quiet -nostats -c:v h264_v4l2m2m -i udp://239.0.1.23:1234 \
  -vf "crop=640:360:0:0,format=bgra" \
  -f fbdev /dev/fb0

The crop=640:360:0:0 is to chop the 1280 x720 full feed into the desired quadrant.
CRT1 Top Left 640:360:0:0 
CRT2 Top Right 640:360:640:0
CRT3 Bottom Left 640:360:0:360 
CRT4 Bottom Right  640:360:640:360 
For some reason, the format is different for CRT2-4. Same command except format=rgb565le

